we are in the middle of a wind drought  for several days now a stiff breeze has  skirted the UK looping around from  Norway across the Atlantic but Western  Europe is there in the middle of it all  and it's almost totally calm it'll stay  like that for a while yet and that  matters because our wind farms are  almost all in Scotland and the North Sea  it's not until Wednesday or even  Thursday that the winds pick up and the  turbines start turning the lack of wind  is having a big impact on how we're  generating our electricity this has been  our Energy Mix over the last year wind  produced around 28 percent gas 42 and  coal just 1.6 but look at the mix from  lunchtime on Monday gas power stations  have been ramped up producing 61 percent  of our electricity wind is down to a  little over three percent about the same  as being generated by coal this snapshot  is unusual but it does show the  limitations of renewable energy in some  weather conditions and winds are likely  to get weaker with global warming these  are the projections on future wind  speeds even if Nations collectively  reach Net Zero soon after 2050 they drop  significantly across the Northern  Hemisphere shown by these shades of blue  a phenomenon called Global stilling and  it's caused by rapidly Rising  temperatures up in the Arctic in the UK  winds are expected to weaken by up to  three percent by 2050 and 10 percent by  the end of the century right now the UK  is taking a big bet on wind we have 14  gigawatts of onshore wind turbines and  around the same offshore but the  government has a Target to increase that  capacity to 50 gigawatts and to turn off  gas and coal power stations by 2035.  however having the turbines doesn't make  them turn as we're finding out now and  although the government also plans to  expand nuclear energy to 24 gigawatts by  2050 that wouldn't fill the gap by the  lack of wind at the moment we will need  to find some other way of storing vast  amounts of energy or importing much more  through long distance cables if we're  going to keep the lights on
 

